@namespace Hasseware.AspNetCore.Components.Bpmn

<div @ref="HostElement"
     class="bpmn-canvas @Class"
     style="@Style"
     tabindex="0"
     role="application"
     aria-label="@(AriaLabel ?? Strings["AriaLabel"])"
     @ondragover="OnDragOver"
     @ondragover:preventDefault="true"
     @ondrop="OnDrop"
     @ondrop:preventDefault="true"
     @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    @*
        JointJS mounts its paper inside this element and nothing else ever renders into it.

        Blazor and a third-party library cannot both manage one element's children: the renderer
        walks its own logical positions, and an <svg> appended by JointJS among them makes it
        apply attributes to the wrong node ("Cannot set attribute on non-element child"). One
        element the library owns entirely, one the renderer owns entirely, and neither has to know
        about the other.
    *@
    @*
        Hidden from assistive technology, deliberately.

        The drawn cells keep their roles and names — a tool inspecting the DOM reads the same words
        a reader hears, which is worth having — but the SVG is not what a screen reader should walk.
        In paint order it is arbitrary, it says nothing about what connects to what, and once the
        paper culls what is off screen it is not even complete. The accessible representation is the
        tree beside it, built from the model; announcing both would say everything twice and mean
        less each time.
    *@
    <div class="bpmn-canvas__paper" aria-hidden="true" @ref="_paperElement"></div>

    @*
        The expand controls live here: real buttons, drawn over the collapsed "+" markers.

        Empty, and filled by the canvas module rather than by the renderer — the same division as
        the paper above, for the same reason. It is outside the paper because it must NOT be
        hidden from assistive technology: section 1.2.1 wants the expand control focusable and
        named, and ARIA gives a descendant no way out of an aria-hidden ancestor, so a button
        inside the paper would be a tab stop a screen reader could not announce.
    *@
    <div class="bpmn-canvas__controls"></div>

    @*
        Overlays that must sit above the paper — the context menu, the inline label editor, the
        contextual actions — render here, as children of the canvas host rather than siblings of
        the editor, so they position against this element's box. The surface cascade is how
        they can tell they are here.
    *@
    <CascadingValue Value="Surface" IsFixed="true">
        @ChildContent
    </CascadingValue>
</div>
